WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.970
My Form Three spent a whole lesson

2
00:00:02.970 --> 00:00:06.910
typing a ten-line program, and most

3
00:00:06.910 --> 00:00:09.150
of them never got it to run.

4
00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:11.880
They were stuck on spelling and

5
00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:14.100
brackets, not on the idea.

6
00:00:14.890 --> 00:00:17.450
So I gave them the lines already

7
00:00:17.450 --> 00:00:19.530
written, cut into strips.

8
00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:23.220
Today: Parsons problems, and

9
00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:24.020
how to make good ones.

10
00:00:25.340 --> 00:00:28.020
A Parsons problem gives pupils the lines

11
00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:30.100
of a working program, mixed up.

12
00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:32.300
They put them in the right order,

13
00:00:32.660 --> 00:00:34.980
and in Python, indent them too.

14
00:00:35.530 --> 00:00:37.990
They’re named after Dale Parsons, who

15
00:00:37.990 --> 00:00:39.650
described them in two thousand and six.

16
00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:42.140
Here is the answer.

17
00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:45.620
The total starts at zero, then the

18
00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:48.160
loop, then the line inside it,

19
00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:51.360
indented, then the print after the loop.

20
00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:54.580
To solve it, pupils must understand what

21
00:00:54.580 --> 00:00:56.860
each line does and where it belongs.

22
00:00:58.290 --> 00:00:59.090
Why do they work?

23
00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:03.240
Pupils focus on the logic, not on typing.

24
00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:05.480
They can’t make spelling mistakes.

25
00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:08.430
A study in two thousand and seventeen

26
00:01:08.430 --> 00:01:11.070
found that Parsons problems took less

27
00:01:11.070 --> 00:01:13.510
time than writing the same code, with

28
00:01:13.510 --> 00:01:14.210
the same learning.

29
00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:17.920
You can add a distractor: a line that

30
00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:19.480
looks right but doesn’t belong.

31
00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:22.570
Make it the mistake pupils really make,

32
00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:26.350
like one equals sign in an if, or a

33
00:01:26.350 --> 00:01:28.150
range that stops one too early.

34
00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:30.620
Spotting it teaches more than

35
00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:31.620
another correct line.

36
00:01:32.910 --> 00:01:33.390
Quick check!

37
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:36.320
Your Parsons problem prints a pass

38
00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:38.700
message when mark is at least fifty.

39
00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:42.100
Which distractor teaches the most?

40
00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:44.930
The single equals sign.

41
00:01:45.590 --> 00:01:47.010
A distractor nobody would

42
00:01:47.010 --> 00:01:48.390
choose teaches nothing.

43
00:01:49.090 --> 00:01:52.010
A real misconception makes pupils stop

44
00:01:52.010 --> 00:01:53.950
and think: which one compares?

45
00:01:54.760 --> 00:01:56.540
Collect distractors from your

46
00:01:56.540 --> 00:01:57.620
pupils’ own mistakes.

47
00:01:59.110 --> 00:02:01.950
Parsons problems need no device at all.

48
00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:04.760
Print the lines on paper, cut them into

49
00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:07.360
strips, and put each set in an envelope.

50
00:02:08.090 --> 00:02:10.490
Pairs arrange the strips on the desk.

51
00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:13.000
Darsi’s printed worksheets have them

52
00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:14.740
ready, with boxes to number.

53
00:02:15.990 --> 00:02:18.670
To design one, start from a short program

54
00:02:18.670 --> 00:02:20.470
that works, four to eight lines.

55
00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:23.140
Check that only one order is

56
00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:25.260
right: two lines that could swap

57
00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:26.840
make an unfair question.

58
00:02:27.450 --> 00:02:29.910
Add one or two distractors, and

59
00:02:29.910 --> 00:02:32.130
decide whether pupils must indent.

60
00:02:33.490 --> 00:02:35.770
In the app, open Sorting race

61
00:02:35.770 --> 00:02:37.810
and watch bubble sort swap

62
00:02:37.810 --> 00:02:39.990
neighbours, pass after pass.

63
00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:43.260
Then, in this lesson, rebuild its

64
00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:45.480
swap step as a Parsons problem.

65
00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:47.780
Seeing the algorithm first

66
00:02:47.780 --> 00:02:49.700
makes the lines make sense.

67
00:02:51.030 --> 00:02:51.770
Your turn.

68
00:02:52.410 --> 00:02:56.210
A Parsons problem has two lines, name

69
00:02:56.210 --> 00:03:00.550
equals Amina and age equals twelve,

70
00:03:01.110 --> 00:03:02.630
that could go in either order.

71
00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:05.260
Is it a fair question?

72
00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:07.200
Not fair.

73
00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:10.640
Remember: a Parsons problem orders

74
00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:12.620
the lines of a working program.

75
00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:16.120
It focuses pupils on the logic, and

76
00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:17.480
takes less time than writing.

77
00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:21.340
Use real mistakes as distractors, one

78
00:03:21.340 --> 00:03:23.460
right order, and paper strips.

79
00:03:24.860 --> 00:03:26.120
Try it in class tomorrow:

80
00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:27.760
make a Parsons set.

81
00:03:28.370 --> 00:03:30.550
Take a program from your last lesson,

82
00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:34.310
print it, cut it into strips, and add one

83
00:03:34.310 --> 00:03:36.210
distractor from a real mistake.

84
00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:38.820
Pairs race to put it in order.
